WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.379
An esteemed collection of actors have played
Batman over the decades, and Robert Pattinson

00:00:05.379 --> 00:00:07.680
is the newest to join their ranks.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.000
How is his Batsuit distinct from the others?

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.060
Keep watching for all the tactical distinctions.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:17.669
Adam West, Michael Keaton, Val Kilmer, George
Clooney, Christian Bale, and Ben Affleck have

00:00:17.669 --> 00:00:22.289
all had their own versions of the Batsuit,
if not multiple variations on it.

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:27.250
Batman's gear is an integral part translating
the more than 80-year-old character from comics

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.250
to the screen.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:33.180
While costume designers get considerable creative
license on other productions, comic book adaptations

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.610
have to walk a fine line between translating
costumes that look great in the fantastical

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.650
worlds of comics into a realistic film version.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.160
"What gives you the right?

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.090
What's the difference between you and me?"

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.580
"I'm not wearin' hockey pads."

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.990
What did costume supervisor Dave Crossman
and concept artist Glyn Dillon do differently

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.050
for "The Batman"?

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.350
Pattinson, who originally screen tested for
the role wearing a version of the Val Kilmer

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.180
Batsuit, told Entertainment Weekly about his
official costume,

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.400
"I felt very different immediately.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.400
It really flowed.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.030
It was so well-designed, so articulated on
all the joints.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.390
It was kind of shocking."

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.810
"You trying to get under my cape, doctor?"

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:14.011
As cool as the Batsuit always looked, freedom
of movement has not always been high on the

00:01:14.011 --> 00:01:16.540
priority list for the costume designers.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.409
Pattinson's version of the suit makes some
accommodations for that.

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:22.180
It has a collar at the top of the cape around
the neck.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.690
While this may look strange to comic book
fans, it gives Batman protection around his

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:29.430
vulnerable neck area while still enabling
him to turn his head.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.950
In his own interview with Entertainment Weekly,
"The Batman" director Matt Reeves explained,

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.400
"Part of the tradition is that when you're
playing Batman [...] you don't just do an

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.600
audition, you do a screen test, and they need
to see you in a Batsuit.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.250
But they don't make you a Batsuit, because
that's a very involved process.

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:46.260
So what you do is you put on an old Batsuit."

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.920
Pattinson struggled with the heat and the
lack of movement in Kilmer's version.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.380
The costume that was custom-made for him,
by contrast, was almost too comfortable.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.380
Pattinson told EW,

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.950
"In the costume fitting, I ended up doing
loads of somersaults and stuff, and then they

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.880
added all those into the fight scenes.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.740
You can do like a couple of somersaults in
it, but it really gets old pretty quickly."

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:10.060
Reeves and his design team seem to have ditched
the solid rubber of past Batsuits, adopting

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.330
something resembling an actual battle suit.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.600
It all fits with the director's vision of
a more realistic, gritty hero fighting for

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:17.600
his life.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.900
Crossman and Dillon worked hard to echo that
sentiment in the suit.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:21.900
Pattinson told EW,

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.410
"There are little bloodstains on it.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.810
There's kind of grazes where it's been hit
with bullets and kind of just the wear and

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.990
tear of someone who's been out fighting every
night.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.950
[...] It looks like a soldier's armor in some
kind of strange parallel universe where you

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.500
have to wear little ears on top of your head
as well."

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.220
But what about Batman's all-important gadgets?

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.550
"Where does he get those wonderful toys?"

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:46.230
While Batman's comics costume places the majority
of his gadgets in his utility belt, Pattinson's

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.940
Batman has a more modular and tactical approach,
with devices and weapons built in everywhere.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:51.940
He said,

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.209
"The grapple gun is, I think, in every single
Batman movie, but I'm not sure how many times

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:58.310
he used them so defensively.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:02:59.459
It's used as a weapon.

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:03.739
[...] It's kind of based on Travis Bickle,
the gun mechanism, which he has in 'Taxi Driver.'"

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:09.180
There's also apparently a unique use for the
bat symbol on his chest we can't wait to see.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.180
Pattinson explained,

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.760
"It's a tool, which he can pop out of the
chest plate, which is kind of amazing, incredibly

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.440
difficult to design as well, and looks great."

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.630
Wait, like in "Superman 2"?

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:19.770
Probably not.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.860
There also seems to be a gadget hidden on
Batman's leg.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.739
During a chase scene with The Penguin, Batman
causes him to flip his car.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.120
There's a visible bulge on Batman's right
thigh.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.120
Is it a weapon?

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.660
A gas mask like soldiers in the field carry?

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:37.629
We'll find out when "The Batman" comes to
theaters March 4, 2022.

